\mt1 Editor’s Introduction
\ip This volume, the Orthodox Liturgical English Bible, brings together two English translations that have long been read apart. The Old Testament is the Septuagint in the translation of Sir Lancelot Charles Lee Brenton, as printed in 1870 and as published online by eBible.org, with the corrections eBible has made to it. Brenton’s own preface, introduction, notes, and appendices are kept, apart from his table of the chapters of Jeremias, together with an introduction to the Apocrypha that is not his but was written for a later edition of his translation, since it describes discoveries made after his death in 1862. The New Testament is the Authorized, or King James, Version in the text of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible of 1873, edited by F. H. A. Scrivener, likewise as published online by eBible.org, together with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. Scrivener’s own critical introduction and appendices were never part of the online text and are not printed here. Both translations are in the public domain outside the United Kingdom. Brenton’s is also in the public domain in the UK, where the right to print and publish the King James Version belongs to the Crown and is licensed only to certain publishers.
\ip It is called Orthodox because its Old Testament is the Septuagint, the Old Testament of the Orthodox Church, with its books in the order of the Church of Greece. It is called liturgical because both translations are in the English of the King James Bible, in which the services of the Church have commonly been put into English.
\ip Brenton’s list of abbreviations, which the editor has supplemented with further abbreviations used in the notes, follows the table of contents. Each testament opens with the front matter of its own translation: Brenton’s preface and introduction and the introduction to the Apocrypha come before Genesis, and the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader before Matthew. Brenton’s notes and supplied passages follow Revelation.
\ip The books of the Old Testament are arranged in the order of the Church of Greece. The books that English Bibles set apart as the Apocrypha are printed in their Greek places, so there is no separate Apocrypha section: the Prayer of Manasses follows 2 Chronicles, 4 Maccabees follows 3 Maccabees, and the additions to Esther and Daniel and Psalm 151 stand within the text. The introduction to the Apocrypha keeps at the front its general account of those books and its paragraph on the second book of Esdras of the English Apocrypha, which is not this edition’s 2 Esdras and is not printed here; its account of each book that is printed here is a footnote where that book or section begins. In both places, book names follow this edition’s titles and spellings; words in square brackets are the editor’s. Brenton’s single book of Ezra and Nehemiah is divided into 2 Esdras and Nehemias, and {brenton MAL 3:19-24} is numbered {bare MAL 4:1-6}, again following Greek practice. Apart from these, the chapter and verse numbers are those of the translations themselves. A few books are renamed in the same spirit: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi, and his four books of Kings are the four books of Kingdoms. Hosea and Nahum, on the other hand, take their familiar English names in place of Brenton’s Osee and Naum. Every book is also given a full title in the manner of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible, whose Genesis is “The First Book of Moses, Called Genesis,” and a short name for the running heads.
\ip Selected English readings from the Alexandrine Text, supplied by Brenton’s notes and Appendix, are printed in the Old Testament where the recorded Swete evidence supports their wording and placement. Nine further Brenton alternatives are compatible with Swete’s text without establishing an Alexandrine–Vatican contrast. Displaced readings appear in footnotes as “Vat.” where that attribution is supported, or “Brenton’s text” where it is not. Promoted passages are removed from the Appendix; doubtful readings remain in notes or the Appendix. The missing verse at {1SA 23:12} is supplied verbatim from the Authorized Version, whose sense agrees with Swete’s Alexandrine reading. The New Testament’s wording is unchanged. Brenton’s footnotes are printed, with a few slips in eBible’s text of them corrected and the names of the five scholars his notes cite written in full; a matching “See” note is replaced by a quotation link. What a note cites, or the front or back matter of either translation, is printed under this edition’s names for the books and by its numbers, in one form. In the notes and in the front and back matter of both translations, abbreviations and numbers follow The Chicago Manual of Style, as “i.e.,” “LXX,” “280 BC,” and “etc.” for “i. e.,” “LXX.,” “b.c. 280,” and “&c.,” and the Authorized Version is called so in full where Brenton writes “A. V.” or “the English Version”; the words are otherwise their authors’. The online text of the Cambridge New Testament has no notes, so the New Testament carries instead the marginal notes of the King James Bible of 1611 as transcribed by Calvin George: the translators’ other possible renderings, their literal glosses of the Greek, and their remarks on its readings. Their marginal cross-references are not included. Words that the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which many Bibles print in italics, are set here in ordinary type. The title pages, the section dividers, the table of contents, and the headings are this edition’s own, and the opinions expressed in the introductions and appendices are those of their original authors.
\ip A quotation link stands at the first verse of each quoting and each quoted passage, and names the complete passage at the other end. Where Turpie (1868) judged that the quotation’s wording follows the Hebrew and the Septuagint alike, or, where the two agree, differs from them only in words and not in whole clauses, the reference is followed by “Heb. and LXX”; where it follows the Hebrew against the Septuagint, by “Heb. against LXX”; and where it follows the Septuagint against the Hebrew, by “LXX against Heb.” A reference with no gloss marks a quotation that he judged to follow neither, or to depart from both by whole clauses, as when a verse is abridged or two passages are joined. These are Turpie’s judgments of the Greek and Hebrew texts, not a comparison of the King James Version with Brenton, and not a word-for-word match.
\ip A link names Brenton’s chapter and verse, which in places differ from the King James Bible’s. Brenton numbers the psalms as the Greek does, most of them one lower, and he counts a psalm’s title as its first verse or two, so that {PSA 33:13-17} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare PSA 33:13-17}. From chapter 25 on, Jeremias arranges its chapters in another order, so that {JER 38:31-34} is the King James Bible’s {kjv bare JER 38:31-34}. Where the Septuagint adds to a verse, Brenton may set the addition apart as a lettered verse, which the King James Bible lacks: the cheerful giver of {2CO 9:7} is found at {PRO 22:8a}. The table that follows this introduction gives the King James Bible’s number for every chapter and verse of the Old Testament that it numbers otherwise. It takes the place of Brenton’s table of the chapters of Jeremias.
\ip The volume was typeset with PTXprint, a free program for setting Bibles, on A5 pages in one column. The text is set in Utopia.
